WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.444
Direct indexing is sometimes appropriate,

2
00:00:02.524 --> 00:00:05.783
especially when surrounding logic has
already proved the index is valid.

3
00:00:05.863 --> 00:00:07.387
But it should not be the only habit.

4
00:00:07.467 --> 00:00:10.410
If an index is influenced by input,
parsing, length calculations,

5
00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:12.171
record structure, or previous state,

6
00:00:12.251 --> 00:00:14.721
checked access can make the intended
behavior clearer.

7
00:00:14.801 --> 00:00:18.375
Methods such as checked lookup let code
handle missing or short data explicitly.

8
00:00:18.455 --> 00:00:20.084
Instead of assuming an index exists,

9
00:00:20.164 --> 00:00:22.739
the code can decide what should happen
when data is absent.

10
00:00:22.819 --> 00:00:26.287
That choice is important for parsers,
command-line tools, network handlers,

11
00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:28.837
and services that process untrusted or
variable input.

12
00:00:28.917 --> 00:00:32.648
Iterators often make buffer access safer
and clearer than manual index arithmetic.

13
00:00:32.728 --> 00:00:36.302
They let the collection manage traversal
while the code focuses on the operation

14
00:00:36.382 --> 00:00:37.170
being performed.

15
00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:39.142
When a loop does not need the numeric
index,

16
00:00:39.222 --> 00:00:42.953
removing manual indexing also removes
opportunities for off-by-one assumptions.

17
00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:45.766
Pattern matching and slice APIs can
express expected shapes.

18
00:00:45.846 --> 00:00:48.999
Code can distinguish empty data, one
element, a fixed header plus body,

19
00:00:49.079 --> 00:00:50.235
or a prefix and remainder.

20
00:00:50.315 --> 00:00:52.207
These patterns make assumptions visible.

21
00:00:52.287 --> 00:00:55.861
They also make failure behavior easier to
review because unsupported shapes are

22
00:00:55.941 --> 00:00:57.728
handled as cases rather than accidents.

23
00:00:57.808 --> 00:01:00.750
Defensive Rust reviews empty, short, long,
and malformed inputs.

24
00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:03.458
It asks what happens at the first element,
the last element,

25
00:01:03.538 --> 00:01:05.693
one past the last element, and no elements
at all.

26
00:01:05.773 --> 00:01:07.192
The goal is not to fear indexing.

27
00:01:07.272 --> 00:01:10.740
The goal is to choose access patterns that
make boundary behavior deliberate.
